---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Scheduled browser jobs with HailBrowser',
    description:
      'Run a goal once, daily, on weekdays or every few hours in a background tab, with a notification at the end and stricter rules for unattended runs.',
    dateModified: '2026-10-30',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'HailBrowser contributors' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/use-cases/recurring-jobs`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Use cases', item: `${SITE.url}/use-cases` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Recurring jobs', item: `${SITE.url}/use-cases/recurring-jobs` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Scheduled browser jobs that run before you sit down"
  description="Schedule a HailBrowser goal once, daily, on weekdays or every few hours. It runs in a background tab, follows stricter rules, and sends a notification with the result."
  type="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="frame">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/use-cases">Use cases</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Recurring jobs</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero__meta">
        <span class="pill pill--ink">Use case 03</span>
        <span class="pill">Once · daily · weekdays · every few hours</span>
      </div>
      <h1>The job is done<br />before you sit down.</h1>
      <p>
        A portal you check every morning, a price you watch, a report you rebuild each Monday. Say it
        once and let Chrome do it in a background tab.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="split split--wide-right">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The prompt</h2>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Every weekday at 8, check my supplier portal for
new invoices and tell me the total. If something
needs a decision, say so instead of guessing.`}
          file="side panel · Chat"
          lang="text"
          label="A goal with a time and a rule"
        />
        <p>
          HailBrowser proposes the schedule in the chat. It is only created when you press
          <strong>Create</strong>, so a page cannot add one behind your back.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>The schedule is stored with <code>chrome.alarms</code> and appears in the schedule list.</li>
          <li>At the chosen time it opens a background tab, runs the goal and closes what it opened.</li>
          <li>A notification reports the result, and the run is saved in History like any other chat.</li>
          <li>If it needs a login, a payment or a CAPTCHA, it stops and tells you rather than waiting silently.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Stricter when nobody is watching</h2>
        <p>
          Scheduled runs follow the same tools but a tighter rule: they never send, buy, post or delete
          anything unless the goal says so. The goal is the permission. “Check the total” does not
          authorise “pay the invoice”.
        </p>
        <p>
          That also means a scheduled job will not push through ambiguity. It reports what needs you,
          which is usually the point.
        </p>

        <h2>What to check</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The first run. Watch it once before you trust it on a schedule.</li>
          <li>The notification text, not just that a notification arrived.</li>
          <li>The queue. One run happens at a time, so a long job at 9 delays the job scheduled for 9:05.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What can go wrong</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Chrome was closed.</strong> The job runs once at the next start, not once for every missed interval.</li>
          <li><strong>The site logged you out.</strong> The run stops and asks, or reports that it could not sign in.</li>
          <li><strong>Another run is in progress.</strong> The job waits in the queue rather than interleaving clicks.</li>
          <li><strong>The site changed.</strong> A moved button costs a decision, not a silent failure; the run reports where it stopped.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="stack" style="align-content:start">
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">Schedules</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Once, at a date and time</li>
            <li>Daily, at a time</li>
            <li>Weekdays, at a time</li>
            <li>Every few hours</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">Related</p>
          <ul class="tool-links">
            <li><a href="/docs/modes#tasks">Tasks mode</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/modes#research">Research depths</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/limits#jobs">Scheduling limits</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="label">One at a time</p>
          <p>
            Every client shares one browser and one debugger. Two runs at once would interleave clicks,
            so the second waits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <section class="section section--tight">
      <nav class="more-links" aria-label="Related">
        <a href="/use-cases/form-flows">
          <span class="label">Next</span>
          <b>Form flows</b>
          <em>Fill the long form without a text model.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/modes">
          <span class="label">Behaviour</span>
          <b>Modes</b>
          <em>Tasks, research and fast mode.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/security">
          <span class="label">Trust</span>
          <b>Security model</b>
          <em>What a scheduled run may do.</em>
        </a>
        <a href="/faq">
          <span class="label">Answers</span>
          <b>FAQ</b>
          <em>Chrome has to be open. Why?</em>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
